from common import *
import platform,sys
with connect() as c:
 counts={t:c.execute(f'SELECT COUNT(*) FROM {t}').fetchone()[0] for (t,) in c.execute("SELECT name FROM sqlite_master WHERE type='table' AND name NOT LIKE 'sqlite_%'")}
 (ROOT/'results/final_database_counts.json').write_text(json.dumps(counts,indent=2))
 schema=['# Database schema','', 'SQLite foreign keys are enabled on every Python and R connection. Coordinates are 1-based inclusive. Gene identifiers remove only the documented terminal `.g.v1.0`; original strings are retained. All tables use primary keys; relationships use foreign keys. Missing metabolite class is NULL.', '', '| Table | Rows | Columns (PK positions) |','|---|---:|---|']
 for t,n in counts.items():
  cols=c.execute(f'PRAGMA table_info({t})').fetchall();schema.append(f"| {t} | {n} | "+', '.join(f'{x[1]} {x[2]}'+(f' [PK{x[5]}]' if x[5] else '') for x in cols)+' |')
 schema+=['','## Relationships and provenance','',
 'sources stores the input path, SHA-256 hash, format and inspected row count, including reference-only comparison outputs. genes links to its GFF source. gene_aliases retains original IDs per source. traits and genes are linked by trait_genes; candidate_records preserves every source token, row order and membership provenance. gene_go links genes and go_terms with its CPBD mapping source. annotation_records preserves every raw CPBD observation and retrieval status.', '',
 'associations stores one trait–SNP observation per source, including original/forced chromosome and P-value. association_genes links observations to genes, preserves reported coordinates/distance/labels, and is checked against GFF coordinates during ETL. association_records preserves every original row as JSON. It is an explicit audit layer; scientific retrieval uses normalized tables. No associations create candidate membership.', '',
 'analysis_runs holds parameters, package session information and manifest hash once per run. enrichment_results contains all tested terms, with significant flag and raw/adjusted P-values, q-values, counts and ratios. enrichment_genes normalizes result gene membership. Ontology and term names are retrieved from go_terms instead of copied into result rows. background_genes is a view of DISTINCT gene_go genes (16,226), not a fabricated genome-wide universe.', '',
 'GO ontology values retain source spelling: BP/CC/MF and QuickGO biological_process/cellular_component/molecular_function. These correspond to three categories but are pooled in the original statistical analysis. Fifteen Current_GO_ID values differ from GO_ID; they remain metadata and are not substituted. There are six failed_read raw CPBD observations and 8,301 no_GO observations; all are preserved.', '',
 '## Indexes and constraints','',
 'Composite primary keys enforce unique trait–gene and gene–GO relationships. Secondary indexes support reverse GO lookup, reverse trait membership, scaffold interval queries and association gene retrieval. Coordinate and P-value CHECK constraints reject invalid values; ETL validates source relationships and fails on inconsistent SNP metadata. SQL parameter values are bound rather than interpolated. indexes.sql contains all named secondary indexes.', '',
 '## Build behavior','',
 'A new database is built in a temporary file, using a transaction for all imports, checked, indexed and atomically renamed. Identical source hashes make a repeated build a no-op. Changed inputs require a new --database target: an existing database is never replaced by the builder. Duplicate candidate and mapping relationships are logged; every candidate source row remains in candidate_records. Missing GO descriptions create explicit NULL metadata and a logged issue. Conflicting gene coordinates or SNP observations abort rather than silently choose a record.', '',
 'The inspected snapshot has no duplicate pairs, invalid coordinates, missing gene coordinates, missing GO metadata or referential-integrity violations. import_counts.json reports the import-only stage; final_database_counts.json includes computed enrichment.']
 (ROOT/'docs/generated/database_schema.md').write_text('\n'.join(schema)+'\n')
benchmark=pd.read_csv(ROOT/'results/sql_performance_benchmark.csv')
pivot=benchmark.pivot(index='operation',columns='engine',values='median_seconds')
text='# Performance report\n\nAll 24 engine/operation outputs matched the independently calculated data.table result after sorting. Times are warm-cache medians, in milliseconds, for 7 batches of 20 repetitions. Loading inputs and constructing databases are excluded.\n\n| Operation | SQLite on disk | R data.table | RAM SQLite indexed | RAM without secondary indexes |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n'
for op,r in pivot.iterrows():text+=f"| {op} | {r['sqlite_disk_indexed']*1000:.3f} | {r['R_data.table']*1000:.3f} | {r['sqlite_memory_indexed']*1000:.3f} | {r['sqlite_memory_no_secondary_indexes']*1000:.3f} |\n"
text+='''\nThese measurements describe this small dataset and these implementations, not a general SQLite advantage. The original workflow provides candidate/annotation retrieval operations; coverage, shared-gene and aggregation comparisons use equivalent data.table implementations rather than claiming a historical benchmark. data.table may create automatic indexes during warm-up. At this scale timer quantization and binding overhead matter.\n\nThe controlled index comparison uses two in-memory copies, retaining primary-key indexes in both. Examine `results/sql_query_plans.csv` alongside timings: reverse gene membership and selective term lookup benefit from secondary indexes, whereas primary-key lookups already have indexes and some aggregation workloads gain little. Returned object sizes are measured; these are not peak process memory. All build and file-loading costs are excluded. No claim is made about end-to-end enrichment speed.\n'''
(ROOT/'docs/generated/performance_report.md').write_text(text)
(ROOT/'results/python_environment.json').write_text(json.dumps({'python':sys.version,'pandas':pd.__version__,'sqlite':sqlite3.sqlite_version,'platform':platform.platform()},indent=2))
# Confirm source hashes after all analysis, without modifying any original file.
manifest=json.loads((ROOT/'docs/generated/input_manifest.json').read_text())
checks=[{'path':r['path'],'unchanged':sha(r['path'])==r['sha256']} for r in manifest]
pd.DataFrame(checks).to_csv(ROOT/'results/source_integrity.csv',index=False)
assert all(r['unchanged'] for r in checks)
print('Finalized documentation; all',len(checks),'source hashes unchanged')
